# Release-signing credentials — TEMPLATE (this file is committed; the real
# android/key.properties is gitignored and the gate fails if one is ever
# tracked). Copy this file to android/key.properties
# and fill it in locally — type the values in your own editor/terminal only;
# never paste passwords into a chat session or a committed file.
#
# Generate the upload keystore first (one-time, OUTSIDE the repo). Run this in
# YOUR OWN terminal. Let keytool PROMPT for the passwords — do not pass them on
# the command line, and do not let a script or an assistant read them out of a
# file. This project's own key was regenerated non-interactively once, reusing a
# password that had already passed through a session transcript; that is the
# specific mistake this paragraph exists to stop being repeated.
#
#   keytool -genkeypair -v -keystore <a path outside the repo>.jks \
#     -alias upload -keyalg RSA -keysize 4096 -validity 10950 \
#     -dname "CN=KaspaVerse, O=KaspaVerse, C=NG"
#
# Then `chmod 600` BOTH the keystore and your filled-in android/key.properties —
# keytool creates the keystore with your umask, which on a typical Linux box is
# world-readable. The gate fails if key.properties is not 0600.
#
# The -dname is deliberately pseudonymous and should stay that way. Android never
# validates its contents and the certificate is self-signed, so nothing depends on
# it being a real identity — but whatever it says ships, readable, inside every
# APK the project ever signs. It carried a legal name and home city until the
# rotation; keep it to the project, not a person.
#
# storeFile is an absolute path OUTSIDE the repo (e.g. ~/keystores/…).
storeFile=/home/CHANGE_ME/keystores/kaspaverse-upload.jks
storePassword=CHANGE_ME
keyAlias=upload
keyPassword=CHANGE_ME
